1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,944
Session seven.

2
00:00:01,224 --> 00:00:02,551
Building for failures.

3
00:00:02,831 --> 00:00:06,863
This session is built around one mind
map, called Failures and Retries.

4
00:00:07,143 --> 00:00:12,254
The core idea: a network request is a chain of
steps, each step fails in its own way,

5
00:00:12,254 --> 00:00:15,380
and the right reaction depends
on which step failed.

6
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,356
Here is the chain.

7
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,485
First, the socket system call.

8
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:21,178
Then a DNS lookup.

9
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:22,374
Then connect.

10
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:23,488
Then write.

11
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:24,601
Then read.

12
00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:26,736
Then the CDN, with its tiers.

13
00:00:27,066 --> 00:00:28,782
Then SSL and TLS.

14
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,284
And finally, HTTP itself.

15
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:33,210
Each step fails in its own way.

16
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:38,296
And the right reaction, whether that is to
retry, fall back, give up, or serve stale,

17
00:00:38,296 --> 00:00:39,935
depends on which step failed.

18
00:00:40,215 --> 00:00:42,300
We will go through the map branch by branch.

19
00:00:43,233 --> 00:00:45,304
Step one: the socket system call.

20
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:49,113
The only failure: you cannot create a socket,

21
00:00:49,113 --> 00:00:52,387
because you are out of file
descriptors or resources.

22
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:57,035
The map's reaction: retry, because after
some time a socket can get free.

23
00:00:57,315 --> 00:01:03,054
socket fails with EMFILE or ENFILE when the
ulimit, one thousand twenty-four by default,

24
00:01:03,054 --> 00:01:03,902
is exhausted.

25
00:01:04,182 --> 00:01:07,142
Sockets in TIME_WAIT or being
closed free up later.

26
00:01:08,066 --> 00:01:09,880
Step two: the DNS lookup.

27
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:11,314
Three ways it fails.

28
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:13,937
The mapping is not available.

29
00:01:14,217 --> 00:01:16,248
There is no record: NXDOMAIN.

30
00:01:16,528 --> 00:01:19,397
The name does not exist, or the
record was deleted.

31
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:22,348
A network issue causes a DNS timeout.

32
00:01:22,628 --> 00:01:27,031
The UDP query to the resolver was
lost, and gethostbyname blocks,

33
00:01:27,031 --> 00:01:28,928
with no timeout you control.

34
00:01:29,266 --> 00:01:33,998
Or the DNS resolved, but was
misconfigured: it returned the wrong IP.

35
00:01:34,278 --> 00:01:37,824
A bad record, a stale hosts entry,
or the wrong resolver.

36
00:01:38,766 --> 00:01:40,051
Step three: connect.

37
00:01:40,331 --> 00:01:42,025
This one has the most branches.

38
00:01:43,066 --> 00:01:45,964
Host unreachable, because of network blockers.

39
00:01:46,244 --> 00:01:50,366
There is no route, or a firewall, security
group or NAT blocks the path.

40
00:01:50,699 --> 00:01:52,153
The port is not listening.

41
00:01:52,433 --> 00:01:56,365
Nothing is bound, so the kernel
answers the SYN with an RST.

42
00:01:56,645 --> 00:01:57,854
Connection refused.

43
00:01:58,134 --> 00:01:59,169
Fast, and loud.

44
00:01:59,499 --> 00:02:02,297
The number of connections is
exceeded on the client.

45
00:02:02,577 --> 00:02:06,026
It is out of file descriptors, or
out of ephemeral ports:

46
00:02:06,026 --> 00:02:10,687
thirty-two thousand seven hundred sixty-
eight to sixty thousand nine hundred

47
00:02:10,687 --> 00:02:15,287
ninety-nine is twenty-eight thousand two
hundred thirty-two per destination,

48
00:02:15,287 --> 00:02:17,889
and TIME_WAIT holds them for sixty seconds.

49
00:02:18,233 --> 00:02:23,414
Or the number of connections is exceeded on
the server: the accept queue is full,

50
00:02:23,414 --> 00:02:27,699
or the fd limit is hit, or the SYN
backlog is full, in a SYN flood.

51
00:02:27,979 --> 00:02:31,568
The behaviour is not portable: it
is refused, or it hangs.

52
00:02:33,099 --> 00:02:36,662
The first is a TCP timer: no response to the SYN.

53
00:02:36,942 --> 00:02:41,361
That can mean there is no server, or a
slow network with packet drops,

54
00:02:41,361 --> 00:02:45,149
or a handshake failure, or simply a
connect or dial timeout.

55
00:02:45,466 --> 00:02:50,079
The SYN is retransmitted with exponential
backoff until the connect timeout,

56
00:02:50,079 --> 00:02:51,414
which gives ETIMEDOUT.

57
00:02:51,694 --> 00:02:55,812
It is silent and slow, and looks
exactly like an overloaded server.

58
00:02:56,133 --> 00:02:58,696
The second: a stale DNS IP entry.

59
00:02:58,976 --> 00:03:03,200
A cached address, from the TTL, the
hosts file, or a client cache,

60
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,631
points at a box that no longer serves.

61
00:03:06,566 --> 00:03:07,635
Step four: write.

62
00:03:08,666 --> 00:03:12,199
A network issue causes packet drops,
and then a write timeout.

63
00:03:12,479 --> 00:03:15,871
The send buffer fills, because the
peer is not acknowledging.

64
00:03:16,199 --> 00:03:17,689
A physical machine shutdown.

65
00:03:17,969 --> 00:03:22,158
The peer is gone, writes eventually
fail with EPIPE or ECONNRESET,

66
00:03:22,158 --> 00:03:25,585
and SIGPIPE kills the process if
you do not handle it.

67
00:03:25,933 --> 00:03:31,395
Some network node in the path shuts down: your
laptop's network, a Wi-Fi access point,

68
00:03:31,395 --> 00:03:32,792
or the server machine.

69
00:03:33,072 --> 00:03:37,281
A TCP connection is the four-tuple, so
a network change kills it.

70
00:03:37,561 --> 00:03:39,477
QUIC connection I Ds fix that.

71
00:03:39,799 --> 00:03:45,093
A rate limit, driven by server business logic:
the server closes, or stops reading,

72
00:03:45,093 --> 00:03:46,432
so your writes block.

73
00:03:46,712 --> 00:03:49,495
That is TCP flow control, or backpressure.

74
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:53,317
Or a rate limit because you
submitted very large content.

75
00:03:53,597 --> 00:03:55,715
Four thirteen, Payload Too Large.

76
00:03:55,995 --> 00:03:58,241
Expect 100-continue asks first.

77
00:03:59,166 --> 00:04:00,470
Step five: read.

78
00:04:01,499 --> 00:04:06,417
Access denied by the server's business logic:
it read the request, and refused.

79
00:04:06,697 --> 00:04:08,421
Four oh one or four oh three.

80
00:04:08,766 --> 00:04:10,712
A read timeout from a network issue.

81
00:04:10,992 --> 00:04:12,812
Nothing arrives within the deadline.

82
00:04:13,092 --> 00:04:15,555
And note: the work may or may not have been done.

83
00:04:15,899 --> 00:04:21,018
A rate limit: four twenty-nine Too Many
Requests, often with a Retry-After header.

84
00:04:21,366 --> 00:04:23,657
Server-side errors: the five hundreds.

85
00:04:23,999 --> 00:04:26,340
Or the server drops the connection silently.

86
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:29,713
Read returns zero, end of file, or ECONNRESET,

87
00:04:29,713 --> 00:04:33,814
because an idle timeout on a load
balancer or NAT dropped it.

88
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:38,228
Step six: the CDN, which adds
its own failure modes.

89
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:44,486
DNS anycast: BGP chooses the wrong or an
unhealthy point of presence,

90
00:04:44,486 --> 00:04:47,058
or routing changes mid-connection.

91
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:50,397
A bad server, from configuration or overload.

92
00:04:50,677 --> 00:04:55,517
For example Fastly on the eighth of June
twenty twenty-one: one config change,

93
00:04:55,517 --> 00:04:59,550
eighty-five percent of the network in
errors, forty-nine minutes.

94
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:05,416
Stale information, from a bug or a configuration:
the cache serves wrong or old content.

95
00:05:05,696 --> 00:05:08,428
Poisoning, bad TTLs, stale playlists.

96
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:13,830
All the other TCP errors discussed above
apply to the CDN's own hops.

97
00:05:14,166 --> 00:05:17,312
And the same errors can occur
between the edge and the tiers,

98
00:05:17,312 --> 00:05:19,375
and between the mid-tier and the origin.

99
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:22,654
Step seven: SSL and TLS.

100
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:29,588
An invalid certificate: expired, which is the
three AM outage, or the wrong hostname,

101
00:05:29,588 --> 00:05:31,320
or an untrusted CA chain.

102
00:05:31,666 --> 00:05:36,807
A protocol mismatch: TLS one point one,
one point two or one point three.

103
00:05:37,087 --> 00:05:39,884
The client and server support no common version.

104
00:05:40,233 --> 00:05:42,894
A cipher mismatch: no common cipher suite.

105
00:05:43,233 --> 00:05:48,665
A pinning mismatch: the app pinned a specific
certificate or key, and the server rotated it.

106
00:05:48,945 --> 00:05:50,031
The app refuses.

107
00:05:50,366 --> 00:05:53,530
An incorrect date or time, on the
server or the client.

108
00:05:53,810 --> 00:05:56,375
The cert appears not yet valid, or expired.

109
00:05:56,700 --> 00:06:01,530
And an underlying TCP timeout,
because TLS runs on TCP.

110
00:06:02,466 --> 00:06:04,133
Step eight: HTTP.

111
00:06:05,166 --> 00:06:11,734
An HTTP protocol version error: five oh five, or
an h2 preface sent to an h1 server,

112
00:06:11,734 --> 00:06:15,331
which gets a five oh one, or an ALPN mismatch.

113
00:06:15,666 --> 00:06:18,443
Business logic: incorrect or missing headers.

114
00:06:18,723 --> 00:06:23,139
App level validation, like a missing auth
header or the wrong content type.

115
00:06:23,466 --> 00:06:26,976
Protocol requirements: incorrect
or missing headers,

116
00:06:26,976 --> 00:06:31,904
like HTTP one point one without a Host
header, which gets a four hundred.

117
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:35,935
An auth failure: four oh one
Unauthorized from a server,

118
00:06:35,935 --> 00:06:39,902
or four oh seven Proxy Authentication Required
from a proxy.

119
00:06:40,833 --> 00:06:42,796
The rest of the HTTP branch.

120
00:06:43,833 --> 00:06:46,905
The four hundreds: denied, not found, bad request.

121
00:06:47,185 --> 00:06:51,014
These are client errors, and retrying
the same request will not help.

122
00:06:51,333 --> 00:06:52,395
The five hundreds.

123
00:06:52,675 --> 00:06:56,941
Five oh two Bad Gateway: the proxy got a
bad response from upstream.

124
00:06:57,221 --> 00:07:01,433
Five oh three Service Unavailable:
overloaded or in maintenance,

125
00:07:01,433 --> 00:07:03,341
and it may carry Retry-After.

126
00:07:03,621 --> 00:07:07,534
Five oh four Gateway Timeout:
upstream did not answer in time.

127
00:07:07,866 --> 00:07:10,392
An OPTIONS preflight that fails: CORS.

128
00:07:10,672 --> 00:07:14,496
The browser's preflight was rejected,
so the request is never sent.

129
00:07:14,833 --> 00:07:19,513
And a read timeout at the server or CDN:
a proxy's upstream timeout.

130
00:07:19,793 --> 00:07:25,936
nginx's proxy_read_timeout and an ALB's idle
timeout both default to sixty seconds.

131
00:07:26,866 --> 00:07:28,508
So which errors should you retry?

132
00:07:28,833 --> 00:07:33,909
Retry the transient errors: timeouts,
connection refused, since it may come back,

133
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:39,110
five oh two, five oh three, five oh four, a
four twenty-nine after the Retry-After,

134
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:41,993
and a socket call that ran out of descriptors.

135
00:07:42,333 --> 00:07:46,361
Do not retry four hundred, four oh one,
four oh three or four oh four.

136
00:07:46,641 --> 00:07:48,782
The same request will fail the same way.

137
00:07:49,062 --> 00:07:53,810
And do not retry anything that is not
idempotent, unless you made it safe to repeat.

138
00:07:54,133 --> 00:07:57,992
Remember that a read timeout means the work
may or may not have been done.

139
00:07:58,272 --> 00:08:00,131
The network is not a function call.

140
00:08:01,066 --> 00:08:02,526
Now the retry toolkit.

141
00:08:02,806 --> 00:08:05,969
These are the concepts the course
refers back to as week seven.

142
00:08:06,249 --> 00:08:08,191
A note: the map gives the failures.

143
00:08:08,471 --> 00:08:12,417
The toolkit is assembled from what the
other sessions say about retries.

144
00:08:13,366 --> 00:08:16,536
Exponential backoff: wait longer
after each failure.

145
00:08:16,816 --> 00:08:19,425
One second, two seconds, four seconds, eight.

146
00:08:19,766 --> 00:08:20,851
You have already seen it.

147
00:08:21,131 --> 00:08:22,317
SYN retransmits.

148
00:08:22,597 --> 00:08:25,472
Wi-Fi's random backoff in CSMA/CA.

149
00:08:25,752 --> 00:08:30,090
And the advice from session three: set a
deadline, then retry with backoff.

150
00:08:31,033 --> 00:08:32,773
But backoff alone is not enough.

151
00:08:33,053 --> 00:08:37,512
If every client backs off the same amount, they
all come back at the same instant.

152
00:08:37,792 --> 00:08:39,129
That is a retry storm.

153
00:08:40,466 --> 00:08:45,350
Without jitter, thousands of clients hit the
server at exactly the same moments,

154
00:08:45,350 --> 00:08:46,754
and amplify the outage.

155
00:08:47,034 --> 00:08:52,766
Fifty million phones retrying a three oh three
Slow Down without backoff is exactly this.

156
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:56,763
With full jitter, you randomise the
wait, so the retries spread out.

157
00:08:57,043 --> 00:09:00,980
The session eight poller uses plus or
minus twenty percent jitter,

158
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:04,261
so fifty million phones do not
poll on the same second.

159
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,037
And the server can help:

160
00:09:06,037 --> 00:09:11,549
Retry-After with a five oh three or a four twenty-
nine tells you exactly when to come back.

161
00:09:12,466 --> 00:09:16,863
A circuit breaker stops you from hammering a
dependency that is already down.

162
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,465
In the closed state, calls flow normally.

163
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:22,355
After repeated failures, the breaker opens.

164
00:09:22,635 --> 00:09:26,014
You stop calling the dependency for
a while, and fail fast.

165
00:09:26,333 --> 00:09:29,999
After a pause, it goes half open, and
probes with a single call.

166
00:09:30,333 --> 00:09:33,074
If the probe succeeds, the breaker closes again.

167
00:09:33,354 --> 00:09:35,119
If it fails, it opens again.

168
00:09:35,399 --> 00:09:41,217
Failover is the sibling idea: primary to
backup, whether that is an origin, a CDN,

169
00:09:41,217 --> 00:09:42,069
or a region.

170
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,564
The toolkit in one table.

171
00:09:44,844 --> 00:09:45,555
Part one.

172
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:49,258
Timeouts and deadlines: never wait forever.

173
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:51,491
Retry with exponential backoff.

174
00:09:51,833 --> 00:09:52,418
Jitter.

175
00:09:52,766 --> 00:09:53,829
A retry storm.

176
00:09:54,166 --> 00:09:57,240
Retry-After, with five oh three
and four twenty-nine.

177
00:09:57,566 --> 00:10:00,972
And idempotency: only retry
what is safe to repeat.

178
00:10:01,252 --> 00:10:03,111
The network is not a function call.

179
00:10:03,391 --> 00:10:06,433
QUIC zero RTT is only for GET and HEAD.

180
00:10:06,713 --> 00:10:12,974
MQTT quality of service one can duplicate
messages, so you apply them by version number.

181
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:14,657
Part two.

182
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:17,064
Which errors to retry.

183
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:19,079
A fallback is not a retry.

184
00:10:19,359 --> 00:10:23,619
It tries something different: another
codec, CDN or rendition.

185
00:10:23,966 --> 00:10:25,038
A circuit breaker.

186
00:10:25,366 --> 00:10:26,095
Failover.

187
00:10:26,433 --> 00:10:28,833
Serve stale, also called stale-if-error.

188
00:10:29,113 --> 00:10:32,409
When the origin fails, the cache is
the last thing standing.

189
00:10:32,733 --> 00:10:37,565
Negative caching: cache errors briefly, one
to two seconds for five hundreds,

190
00:10:37,565 --> 00:10:39,447
to shield a struggling origin.

191
00:10:39,727 --> 00:10:41,596
Almost never for four oh fours.

192
00:10:41,933 --> 00:10:45,119
Graceful degradation, or who
decides what is worse.

193
00:10:45,399 --> 00:10:47,626
A stall is worse than a lower rendition.

194
00:10:47,966 --> 00:10:52,411
The thundering herd on recovery: everything
reconnects or refills at once.

195
00:10:52,733 --> 00:10:56,362
And feature flags: turn features off
at runtime to shed load.

196
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:00,777
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session seven.

197
00:11:19,299 --> 00:11:20,549
That is session seven.

198
00:11:20,829 --> 00:11:24,245
Last one: session eight, streaming
video at large scale.

